    "topic_code": "1.1",
    "topic_title": "Structure of Water and Hydrogen Bonding",
    "stem": "On a hot day, evaporation of sweat lowers a runner's skin temperature. Which explanation best connects the molecular structure of water to this cooling effect?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "Water has a high heat of vaporization, so sweat evaporating from the skin removes heat and cools the body.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This is the common point loss of naming a property without its molecular cause. The statement is true, but it never says that polarity produces hydrogen bonds or that those bonds are what absorb heat as they break. Fix: Check that your explanation includes all three links: polarity, then hydrogen bonds, then the biological effect."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "As sweat evaporates, the polar covalent O–H bonds inside each water molecule break, and breaking these bonds absorbs heat from the skin.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This answer confuses bonds within a molecule with attractions between molecules. Evaporation separates intact water molecules by breaking the hydrogen bonds between them, and the covalent O–H bonds stay intact, so water vapor is still H₂O. Fix: Ask whether a process changes the molecule itself or only separates whole molecules from one another."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "Because water is polar, hydrogen bonds form between molecules as sweat evaporates, and forming these bonds carries heat away from the skin.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This answer reverses the energy direction. Evaporation breaks hydrogen bonds rather than forming them. Breaking bonds absorbs energy, while forming bonds releases energy. Fix: Decide whether bonds are breaking or forming, then check whether that step absorbs or releases heat."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "Water molecules are polar, so they form hydrogen bonds with one another; a large amount of heat must be absorbed from the skin to break these bonds as sweat evaporates, which cools the skin.",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "This answer completes the full chain. Polarity leads to hydrogen bonding between molecules, which gives water a high heat of vaporization. Breaking those bonds during evaporation absorbs heat from the body surface, producing evaporative cooling."
     }
    ]
   },
